{"author":{"id":"3f8bad4585cb0f07c6570d9e31f06264af3b0abf48efbc5a44f56df5e7a9cd45"},"ops":[{"type":6,"timestamp":1790508952,"nonce":"5b2j14n0O/7kGTL1+Fbq5Oh+53M=","target":"110d0f03432984c3ca912763a7593721e45a2c06adfbfd6a82de8dba0e2c57aa","message":"2026-09-27 implementation survey and corrected boundary: Zyntax's current `wasm_backend` emits standalone runtime modules for individual HIR functions. It does not emit relocatable wasm objects that Ash's linker can consume. Its LLVM AOT-via-object path emits a host object, links a host dylib, and dlopens it; it has no portable target/object API for wasm32 yet.\n\nCaribou's AOT coordinator is being changed to collect language artifacts and pass their objects to Ash's one final linker. A declared frontend with no object emitter is refused by name instead of being omitted or interpreted. To complete this issue, Zyntax needs a target-aware relocatable object emitter, `caribou::link` exports, and a frontend call adapter for each supported machine type. Python, Lua, and ZynML then use that same artifact contract.\n\nThe earlier notes about handing final Zyntax wasm modules to a custom linker and statically linking `.zrtl` plugins are superseded: Ash owns the final link, and native plugins/`.zrtl` dependencies remain side modules outside the language-object graph.","files":null}]}